\n\\nThe Problem 4 on chromatically optimal rigid graphs has positive solution. The key ingredient in this is the following result which was first isolated by Rödl and myself in [122]. The result holds for general finite structures. We formulate it just for graphs.\\n\\n**Theorem 6 (Sparse incomparability lemma SIL [122])**  \\nLet \\\\(k,\\\\ell\\\\) be positive integers. Then for every graph \\\\(G\\\\) there exists a graph \\\\(G'\\\\) with the following properties:\\n\\n1. \\\\(G'\\\\) contains no cycles of length \\\\(\\\\le \\\\ell\\\\)  \\n   (i.e. the girth of \\\\(G'\\\\) is \\\\(>\\\\ell\\\\))\\n\\n2. \\\\(G' \\\\to G\\\\)\\n\\n3. For every graph \\\\(H\\\\) with at most \\\\(k\\\\) vertices \\\\(G' \\\\to H\\\\) iff \\\\(G \\\\to H\\\\).\\n\\nPutting intuitively, despite of the fact that \\\\(G'\\\\) is much sparse than \\\\(G\\\\), it cannot be distinguished from \\\\(G\\\\) by the existence of homomorphism into small graphs. (Note that we do not consider counting analogs of this result. This leads to different theory [13] which goes back to Lovász pioneering paper [83]. This in turn inspired both Lovász [84] and Müller [91] work on Ulam’s conjecture.)\\n\\nSparse incomparability lemma holds (with the analogous proof) for relational structures and has many applications (see recent [23]). For example it yields an easy proof of\\n\\n**Theorem 7 (Graph density)**  \\nLet \\\\(G_1,G_2\\\\) be graphs satisfying \\\\(G_1 \\\\to G_2\\\\) and \\\\(G_2 \\\\not\\\\to G_1\\\\) (i.e. \\\\(G_1 < G_2\\\\) in the homomorphism order). Let \\\\(G_2\\\\) be non-bipartite. (Thus we are, up to homomorphism equivalence, excluding the single case: \\\\(G_1 = K_1, G_2 = K_2\\\\).)  \\nThen there exists a graph \\\\(G\\\\) such that\\n\\n\\\\[\\nG_1 \\\\to G \\\\to G_2,\\n\\\\]\\n\\\\[\\nG_2 \\\\not\\\\to G \\\\not\\\\to G_1\\n\\\\]\\n\\n19--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nConstraints, MMSNP and expander relational structures - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/0706.1701v2)\\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 19.4 years ago; Crawled: last month; # • BIB • PDF • ARXIV • ... We give a poly-time construction for a combinatorial classic known as Sparse Incomparability Lemma, studied by Erdos, Lovasz, Nesetril, Rodl and others: We show that every Constraint Satisfaction Problem is poly-time equivalent to its restriction to structures with large girth.\\n\\n## Constraints, MMSNP and expander relational structures\\n\\nGábor Kun\\n\\nPublished: 2007-06-12 | Updated: 2013-03-13\\n\\nComments: 12 pages, 1 figure, Combinatorica, to appear\\n\\nNote:\\n\\nDOI: 10.1007/s00493-013-2405-4\\n\\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\\n\\n### Abstract\\n\\nWe give a poly-time construction for a combinatorial classic known as Sparse Incomparability Lemma, studied by Erdos, Lovasz, Nesetril, Rodl and others: We show that every Constraint Satisfaction Problem is poly-time equivalent to its restriction to structures with large girth. This implies that the complexity classes CSP and Monotone Monadic Strict NP introduced by Feder and Vardi are computationally equivalent. The technical novelty of the paper is a concept of expander relations and a new type of product for relational structures: a generalization of the zig-zag product, the twisted product.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nREFERENCES\\n\\n[1] S.M. Hedetniemi, S.T. Hedetniemi, (https://candc.upjs.sk/history/24abs.pdf)\\nciteturn2search19 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2.2 years ago; Hedetniemi, S.T. ... Lastly, if time permits, we consider triangle-free graphs.\\nHenceforth, we refer to this statement as the Borodin-Kostochka conjecture. If true, we remark that the conjecture would be the best possible. The most significant progress towards the Borodin-Kostochka conjecture has been made by Reed, who showed that it holds for graphs with sufficiently large degree [2].\\n\\nIn th